process-worker-teams.m's workers on the machine's GPU (docs/INPROGRESS.md,
step 4 of the demo, (m1)): with share(K), K kinds that run loops across
teams hold a K'th of the kernel's teams each, at once, and their loops'
reductions are right; without it the second would find no team
(process-loops-gpu.test). With a share of 2, a third finds none.
REQUIRES: amdgpu-gpu

RUN: merkc -O2 -fopenmp -fdo-loops -fprocess-declarations --offload-arch=%offload-arch %libomp-flags %S/process-worker-teams.m -o %t2
RUN: %gpu-env %t2 gpu 2 | FileCheck %S/process-worker-teams.m --match-full-lines --strict-whitespace --check-prefix=TWO
RUN: %gpu-env %t2 gpu 4 | FileCheck %S/process-worker-teams.m --match-full-lines --strict-whitespace --check-prefix=FOUR
RUN: not %gpu-env %t2 gpu-third 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=THIRD
THIRD: Software Error: process.spawn: GPU 0 has no team for the loops of the process kind worker: the module process_worker_teams's kernel there has {{[1-9][0-9]*}} teams, and none is free of processes, which a kind that runs loops across teams takes as its workers

What the loops made goes with each worker's arena when it ends.
RUN: %gpu-env MERK_CHECK_MESSAGES=1 MERK_CHECK_DEVICE_MEMORY=1 %t2 gpu 4 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=FREED
FREED: Mercury runtime: 0 messages are neither read nor freed
FREED-NEXT: Mercury runtime: 0 chunks of the devices' arenas and 0 rings are allocated
